{"step_index":79,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-03T22:47:22Z","content":"Priyanshu,\n\nHere are the direct answers. \n\n**1. What is Manhwa?**\nManhwa is a Korean comic book. It is the Korean version of Japanese manga. The main differences are that manhwa is designed for smartphones (you read it by scrolling down vertically) and it is drawn in full color. Famous examples include *Solo Leveling* and *Tower of God*. \n\n**2. What does \"Source 200-400 panel images\" mean?**\nA \"panel\" is one single drawing or picture box inside a comic. \n\nTo make a 5-hour YouTube video, you cannot just play an AI voice over a black screen. You have to show the actual comic art while the voice tells the story. \n\n\"Sourcing 200-400 panel images\" means you must go to the website where the comic is published, manually screenshot or download 200 to 400 separate pictures, crop them, and put them into your video editor. You then have to sync those pictures so they appear exactly when the AI voice is talking about that specific scene. \n\nAI cannot do this step reliably for you. An AI script generator doesn't know which specific image goes with which sentence, and automated scrapers often grab the wrong images or include messy text bubbles. You have to manually find, download, and organize hundreds of pictures per video. That is why it takes hours of manual labor.","thinking":"**Clarifying the Source**\n\nI'm currently focused on dissecting a user's straightforward request regarding a specific image source. The question centers on identifying \"Source 200-400 panel\". I need to understand its context within the artifact to accurately address it.\n\n\n**Defining the Terminology**\n\nI've moved on to framing the response. Priyanshu needs a clear explanation. My goal is to define \"manhwa\" and \"panel images\" in a direct manner, avoiding any unnecessary flattery. The response aims to illuminate why sourcing 200-400 panels would be labor-intensive, and the mental sandbox simulation indicates this is the best path.\n\n\n**Refining the Explanation**\n\nI'm now honing the draft, streamlining it for clarity and directness. The goal is to articulate the concept of Manhwa and panel images concisely. I am prioritizing a simple structure: a straightforward answer followed by a brief rationale. The explanation will avoid embellishments, sticking to factual information directly related to the task's demands and effort. I'm double-checking for any unwanted jargon.\n\nI'm now drafting the response to Priyanshu: I'll clarify what \"Manhwa\" means, explaining its digital and colorful nature. Then, I will address what \"Source 200-400 panel images\" signifies â€“ manually locating, screenshotting, and integrating images into the video editor to match the narration's timing. I'll make sure to convey the manual effort required in sourcing these individual visual elements.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Response**\n\nI'm now putting the finishing touches on the response. The focus is to keep it factual and direct, cutting out any unnecessary fluff. The draft is designed to be blunt and informative, prioritizing clarity over anything else. I'm focusing on Priyanshu's specific inquiry. The goal is to ensure the response remains practical. I've re-drafted the section to be clear, and now I'm making sure it's perfect. The challenge is to describe why I cannot scrape panels, without human review.\n\n\n"}
